WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.480
Many scientists work on theoretical concepts.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.149
But James Clerk Maxwell’s work was unique
because it led to the development of practical

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:10.170
inventions like the TV.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.960
He changed the world forever.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.580
Those aren’t my words, but the words of
Albert Einstein.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.880
Einstein was referring to Maxwell’s most
famous discovery: that magnetism and electricity

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.260
were linked.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.150
Without this understanding, many of the technologies
we rely on would not exist.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.110
Before we delve into his groundbreaking discovery,
Maxwell's scientific career had begun long

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.910
before at the age of 14 when he published
his first academic paper.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:42.290
It described an unusual way of drawing geometric
curves using twine.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:46.910
A prominent professor at Edinburgh University
described it as “ingenious”.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:52.180
The paper was important not for its content,
but for the effect it had on Maxwell.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.829
It put him on a path to a career in mathematics.

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:02.470
Maxwell was born in Edinburgh on June 13,
1831, and grew up in the country, on a big

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.939
estate his parents built in southwest Scotland.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:08.840
He had an older sister who died in infancy.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.760
His mother was nearly 40 when he was born.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.090
As a little boy, he was fascinated by how
things worked.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:20.210
When he was two, he pulled a rope in one room
and was amazed that a bell rang in another.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.210
Like magic.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.700
Or, rather, like science.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.810
Then he discovered the wires hidden in the
walls.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.490
His mum described him as inquisitive; writing,
“show me how it dooes” is never out of

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.490
his mouth”.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.280
As a boy, he could recite long passages of
John Milton’s poems.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.490
He loved poetry and wrote his own later his
life.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:47.110
His memory was so good that he could recite
all 176 verses of Psalm 119.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:52.020
He was a devout Christian - a man of God and
also a man of science.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.260
He wrote, "Science is but an image of the
truth, and should speak only so far as it

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.700
is spoken to."

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.909
In other words, it cannot provide all pieces
of the puzzle.

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:07.979
His mother was responsible for his early education,
but when he was eight she died of abdominal

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:12.030
cancer, the same disease that would take his
own life.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.440
A private tutor didn’t work out either;
he reportedly beat Maxwell and chided him

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.709
for being slow.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:22.530
Then, when Maxwell was 10, he was sent to
the prestigious Edinburgh Academy.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.540
But he had a hard time fitting in; he was
teased for his homemade shoes, strange country

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.180
dress, and his strong Galloway accent.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.590
His peers called him “Daftie” which is
Scottish slang for “silly” or “foolish”.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:36.900
He was anything but.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.010
However, his peers couldn’t understand his
brilliance; Maxwell would draw diagrams they

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.140
didn’t understand, and make mechanical models
that didn’t make sense to them.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.769
Later, at the University of Edinburgh, he
still struggled to fit in.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:54.860
It wasn’t until he arrived at the University
of Cambridge that his talents began to be

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.160
recognized.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.840
His math tutor at Cambridge, William Hopkins,
is reported to have said that Maxwell was

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.011
the most extraordinary man he had ever met.

00:03:03.011 --> 00:03:04.030
“...he is unquestionably the most extraordinary
man I have met with, in the whole range of

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.030
my experience…”

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.239
When Maxwell later applied for a teaching
position at Marischal College in Aberdeen,

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:12.650
Scotland, to be closer to his father whose
health was deteriorating, Hopkins wrote this

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.420
glowing reference letter:

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:19.800
“His mind is devoted to the prosecution
of scientific studies, and it is impossible

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.480
that he should not become (if his life is
spared) one of the most distinguished men

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.830
of science in this or any other country.”

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.879
Maxwell became the Chair of Natural Philosophy
at Marsichal College - what they used to call

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:33.879
physics.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:37.689
He headed the entire department at the age
of 25.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:42.689
But his dad would never see him in this role;
he passed away before he took up the position,

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:46.189
which was a terrible loss as they were very
close.

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:49.680
Maxwell married the daughter of the head of
Marsichal College who was seven years his

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.680
senior.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.769
Their marriage was childless but very fruitful.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:58.099
Katherine helped him with experiments, including
his famous ones on color.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:02.500
He realized that the human eye can only see
red, blue, and green light.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.110
Any other colors we see are a mixture of these.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.230
Maxwell was the first to show that any color
can be produced by combining three primary

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.890
colors in appropriate proportions.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:18.019
He calculated the exact amount of red, blue,
and green needed to make any other color.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.810
And displayed it on a color triangle.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.940
This led to a method for creating color photographs.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.440
He proposed that a color image could be created
by taking three separate photographs each

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.110
with a different color filter: red, green,
and blue.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:37.500
By superimposing the images together, the
full-color image could be reconstructed.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.800
His colleague, Thomas Suton, applied this
method to create the world’s first color

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.700
photograph of a tartan ribbon in 1861.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.820
While at Marischal College, he also spent
two years solving a problem that had stumped

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.990
scientists for 200 years, ever since Galileo
first observed Saturn’s rings.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.370
What were Saturn’s rings made of?

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.000
Nobody had any idea.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.660
Scientists thought they might have been solid,
but if they were, the rings should have been

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.169
breaking up or crashing into the planet.

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:10.620
Maxwell used math to show that the only way
the rings could remain relatively stable is

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.850
if they consisted of many small particles
each independently orbiting Saturn.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:20.699
When the Voyager probes flew by Saturn more
than 100 years later, in the 1980s, and sent

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:24.110
back images, they proved he was right.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:29.320
Despite Maxwell’s stellar scientific reputation,
he lost his job when Marsichal College merged

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.740
with the neighboring King’s College to form
the University of Aberdeen, and his position

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:35.440
became redundant.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.259
He applied for a Chair at the University of
Edinburgh but lost out to his childhood friend,

00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:42.150
physicist Peter Tait.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.610
Fortunately, his resumé impressed King’s
College London which appointed him Chair of

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.729
Natural Philosophy in 1860.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:54.509
Before taking up his new position, he suffered
from an attack of smallpox which nearly killed

00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:55.509
him.

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:58.210
He credited his wife with saving his life.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:02.430
A few years later, it would be Maxwell who
nursed his wife back to health when she fell

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:03.690
very ill.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:08.120
Maxwell’s biographer and friend described
their marriage as one “...of unexampled

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.819
devotion.”

00:06:09.819 --> 00:06:11.870
His years in London were his most productive.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.210
In fact, that is an understatement.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:18.880
Einstein, Richard Feynman, and many other
scientists consider Maxwell’s work during

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.500
this period the greatest discovery of the
20th century.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.790
Maxwell discovered that magnets attracting
or repelling each other, or electricity making

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.470
a lightbulb turn on, are connected.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:36.290
He built on the work of self-educated physicist
Michael Faraday who had shown that a moving

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.230
magnet could make electricity flow through
a coil of wire.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:46.550
This mysterious interaction between magnetism
and electricity was called “electromagnetism”.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:52.069
Maxwell worked out that varying electric and
magnetic forces could create waves of energy

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:55.170
that travel through space without any wires.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:58.220
Like ripples across the surface of the water.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:02.900
He discovered that electromagnetic waves traveled
at the speed of light.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:07.030
Just as there’s a range of different colors
of light, he theorized that light was just

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:10.800
one type of a whole range of electromagnetic
waves.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.389
And that there are other types that the human
eye can’t see: radio waves, microwaves,

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:17.389
x-rays.

00:07:17.389 --> 00:07:22.289
This paved the way for the radio, microwaves
to heat food, and x-ray machines - pretty

00:07:22.289 --> 00:07:28.030
much any modern electrical or electronic technology
which relies on the transmission of electromagnetic

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.560
waves through the air to send and receive
signals.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.729
In 1865, Maxwell described all that was known
about the relationship between electricity

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:41.330
and magnetism in a set of equations that now
bear his name.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:47.020
Maxwell’s equations also gave Einstein the
tools to develop his most famous theory.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:51.690
Einstein showed that the speed of light never
changes no matter the reference frame, which

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.080
Maxwell's equations helped establish.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.509
For example, if I were to take this ball and
throw it toward you at 20 miles an hour, if

00:07:58.509 --> 00:08:02.400
you were running toward me, the ball would
appear to approach you more quickly, and if

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.129
you were to run away from me, then the ball
would appear to approach you less quickly.

00:08:06.129 --> 00:08:08.610
But that’s not the case with light.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:13.430
If I fire a laser beam at you, regardless
of whether you’re running toward me, away

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:19.390
from me, or if you're standing still, it’ll
appear to approach you at the same speed.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:22.379
This revolutionized our understanding of the
universe.

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:26.580
It’s no wonder when someone pointed out
Einstein stood on the shoulders of the work

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:30.020
of Newton, he reportedly corrected them by
saying, “No, I don’t.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.070
I stand on the shoulders of Maxwell.”

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.310
He even had a portrait of Maxwell hanging
in his office.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:42.760
In 1871, Maxwell became the first Cavendish
Professor of Physics, one of the most senior

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:46.590
faculty positions in physics at Cambridge
University.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.570
He was in charge of developing the Cavendish
Laboratory, which has since produced more

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.089
than 30 Nobel laureates.

00:08:53.089 --> 00:08:58.320
Despite his many scientific contributions,
Maxwell was relatively unknown outside of

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.490
academia.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:05.230
His work was largely foundational; he helped
plant the seed for future discoveries.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:08.390
He also died when he was relatively young.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:14.690
In April 1879, he began having difficulty
swallowing, a symptom of abdominal cancer

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:18.960
which took his mother’s life and would take
his at the same age.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.260
Toward the end of his life, Maxwell told a
Cambridge colleague:

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.640
“I have been thinking how very gently I
have always been dealt with.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.130
I have never had a violent shove all my life.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:35.710
The only desire which I can have is like David
(in reference to King David from the Bible)

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:42.060
to serve my own generation by the will of
God, and then fall asleep.”

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:48.440
Maxwell died on November 5, 1879, at the age
of 48.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:54.190
He was buried in a churchyard not far from
where he grew up, alongside his wife and parents.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.260
His headstone is a simple one, not what you
might expect for a man of his stature.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:05.269
Despite his immense contributions to science,
he received few public honors during his lifetime.

00:10:05.269 --> 00:10:09.010
But over time, he has been increasingly recognized
and celebrated.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.300
There’s a popular saying that references
his brilliance.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:19.250
“And God said…Maxwell’s equations…and
there was light.”

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:24.000
Maxwell used math to explain the fundamental
principles of electricity and magnetism that

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.790
paved the way for countless innovations.

